Subject: [PATCH 0/3] Archive messages that need no answer
To: Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com>

A message leaves the inbox only when you reply to it, so a thread
never closes. Someone always has the last word. This series adds a
single shared archive: anyone can move a message's file, unchanged,
into maildir/archive/, and notmuch new then tags it archived and drops
it from the inbox for everyone. Moving the file back undoes it.

Patch 1 derives the tag in mail-tag.sh. Patch 2 documents archiving in
the workflow and relaxes the two rules that would have rejected it:
the moderator's "only adds messages" check, and the rule that a
message file is never renamed. Patch 3 lets the process-email skill
archive a message instead of replying when nothing is asked.

Tested on a scratch copy of the list. Archiving clears inbox, the tag
survives a database rebuild, unarchiving restores inbox, and a replied
message keeps replied. In jj the move is a single rename, and two
people archiving the same message merge without conflict.

The thread "need some way to close the loop on threads" [1] chose a
shared archive dir over notmuch-git(1) for now. The reply there gives
the experiment's results.

